  "findings": [
    {
      "category": "ssrf",
      "severity": "HIGH",
      "files": ["src/origin.js", "src/fetcher.js"],
      "evidence": "The intended trusted origin is https://trusted.example, but src/origin.js enforces it with url.startsWith('https://trusted.example'), a textual prefix check rather than an origin check. src/fetcher.js then passes the attacker-controlled req.query.url directly to fetch(url). The prefix check is bypassable because the trusted string can appear outside the host component, while fetch() resolves a different host. Confirmed bypass payloads that pass allowedPrefix() but fetch an attacker-controlled origin: (1) subdomain confusion 'https://trusted.example.evil.com/x' (prefix matches, host is trusted.example.evil.com); (2) userinfo confusion 'https://trusted.example@evil.com/x' and 'https://trusted.example:80@evil.com/x' (prefix matches the userinfo segment, but fetch() sends the request to host evil.com). The userinfo form can also target internal addresses (e.g. 'https://trusted.example@169.254.169.254/...') for internal SSRF. Net effect: a user-supplied URL reaches the network request with no effective origin restriction, yielding server-side request forgery to arbitrary HTTPS hosts.",
      "remediation": "Replace the prefix check with strict origin validation: parse the URL with new URL(url), reject any embedded userinfo (parsedUrl.username || parsedUrl.hostname must be clean), assert parsedUrl.protocol === 'https:' and parsedUrl.hostname === 'trusted.example' (exact match, no substring/prefix). Optionally pin parsedUrl.port. Only then call fetch(url); do not pass user input to fetch based on string matching."
    }
  ],
  "scanned_files": ["src/origin.js", "src/fetcher.js"],
  "source_modified": false
}
"}

